    "body": "## Overview\n\nAllow the CuTeDSL DeepSeek gather kernel to accept 16-byte-aligned cache block strides. This fixes rejection of valid packed V4.1 MXFP8 caches during MRV2 warmup.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Relax only the block-stride specialization from 32 to 16 bytes, matching the kernel's existing 128-bit copies. The 32-byte base-pointer alignment is unchanged.\n- Extend the existing writer/FlashMLA/gather comparison to include odd row padding; retain all numerical assertions and the three record formats.\n\n## Validation\n\nNative NVIDIA B300, Torch 2.14.0. Tests used a task-private FF01 runtime with the reviewed source/test overlays; the affected kernel, cache utility and original test bytes match the PR base. This is not a full build of current main.\n\n\ne.g. from a configured vLLM test environment:\n\n~~~bash\nuv run --no-project --python .venv/bin/python python -m pytest tests/kernels/test_compressor_kv_cache.py::test_v41_cache_writers_match_flashmla_decode -q\nuv run --no-project --python .venv/bin/python python -m pytest tests/kernels/test_compressor_kv_cache.py::test_dequantize_and_gather_k_cache -q\n~~~\n\n| Check | Original kernel | Changed kernel |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Six V4.1 cases (same parametrized test in both arms) | 4 passed, 2 failed: odd-padding swa_fp8 and compressed_fp8 | 6 passed |\n| Existing V4 gather vs PyTorch reference, separate invocation | Not rerun | 2 passed |\n| Actual tiny MRV2 LLM startup + two-token generation | Warmup fails on the same stride divisibility check | Starts and generates two tokens |\n\nThe engine diagnostic used a three-layer DeepSeek-V4.1 configuration with the model's first-three-layer source topology (compress_ratios=[0,0,2], KV/index source layer 2, Engram layer 1), reduced dimensions/tables, dummy weights, BF16, TP=1, text input, eager execution and explicit FLASHMLA_SPARSE_DSV41. Only this kernel module changed in the successful engine arm. It is a startup/generation diagnostic, not official-checkpoint accuracy or production CUDA-graph qualification. No speedup is claimed.\n\nRuff check/format, diff check, and seven applicable repository checks passed (test tethering, SPDX, lazy imports, forbidden imports, CUDA calls, config and boolean-context-manager checks). Full pre-commit and upstream CI were not completed locally. Dependency warnings were retained. The existing B200 miscellaneous-kernels CI selection includes this test file.\n\n## Details\n\nAn MXFP8 record is 528 bytes. An odd number of padded rows can therefore produce a block stride divisible by 16 but not 32, even when the allocation's base pointer is 32-byte aligned. The current fake-tensor stride constraint rejects that layout before the gather executes. The existing copies require 16-byte alignment; lowering this stride constraint leaves the base alignment and data/scale addressing unchanged.\n\nThe additional padding case is accepted by FlashMLA decode and the Triton gather; after the fix, CuTeDSL agrees with them under the existing assertions. The existing aligned cases and NVFP4 control also pass.\n\nAI assistance was used. The human submitter reviewed and confirmed understanding of every changed line. Open-PR searches for dequant gather, CuTeDSL stride and the exact module name found no matching fix. The broad SM12x PR #41834 still has the 32-byte stride constraint and does not address this change. This is an independently reproduced startup bug, not a performance cleanup.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's /pr-checklist skill.\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimal specialization change; no new abstraction or backend.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Native red/green kernel and actual engine diagnostics; coverage limits documented above.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Existing test reused; unrelated formatting avoided.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Root cause, evidence, duplicate